window.PHI_GLOSSARY = [{"t": "speculative and experimental natural philosophy", "d": "The two camps of early modern science. By the 1690s the experimental side had hardened into opposition to hypotheses, which is the position she refused.", "w": 7, "f": ["speculative and experimental natural philosophy"]}, {"t": "tempered equality of intellectual authority", "d": "Take seriously anyone with the relevant expertise, and dismiss nobody for gender, race or class. Tempered means expertise still counts.", "w": 6, "f": ["tempered equality of intellectual authority"]}, {"t": "social construction, the three reminders", "d": "Whatever else it claims, it reminds us that science and technology are social, that they are active, and that they do not run straight from nature to ideas about nature.", "w": 4, "f": ["social construction, the three reminders", "social construction"]}, {"t": "appreciation in place of understanding", "d": "Giordano's charge that public understanding of science has mostly meant getting the public to like science and trust scientists. Understanding would mean knowing enough to judge a piece of research, including judging against it. A programme that counts an informed objector as a failure was measuring appreciation.", "w": 7, "f": ["appreciation in place of understanding"]}, {"t": "infanticide, and why it matters here", "d": "A male langur that takes over a troop kills the previous male's infants, because a mother who stops nursing becomes fertile sooner. It is the main threat to an infant's life, and it is what makes mating with several males pay.", "w": 5, "f": ["infanticide, and why it matters here", "infanticides", "infanticide"]}, {"t": "getting in, staying in, getting on", "d": "Sara Delamont's three problems, and the three stages the chapter uses in place of the pipeline.", "w": 4, "f": ["getting in, staying in, getting on", "getting in"]}, {"t": "the Berger and Luckmann conditions", "d": "What it takes to construct a social X: knowledge of X shapes behaviour toward X, knowledge of X is common, and knowledge of X is transmitted. Then X cannot be wished away, and so it exists.", "w": 4, "f": ["the Berger and Luckmann conditions"]}, {"t": "her four constraints on hypotheses", "d": "They must generate experiments; one well-established contrary observation rules one out; a good one explains more than it was built for; and a hypothesis can be true in one part and false in another.", "w": 7, "f": ["her four constraints on hypotheses"]}, {"t": "the social value management ideal", "d": "Kourany's name for Longino's proposal. Values are not removed from science; they are made explicit and argued about in public.", "w": 6, "f": ["the social value management ideal"]}, {"t": "paradigm, as disciplinary matrix", "w": 3, "f": ["paradigm, as disciplinary matrix", "paradigm, as exemplar", "paradigms", "paradigm"], "d": "<b>paradigm, as disciplinary matrix.</b> Everything a scientific community shares: its equations, its models, its values, and its list of problems worth solving. <b>paradigm, as exemplar.</b> A concrete solved problem that students work through and then imitate. Kuhn said this was the sense he cared most about. <b>paradigm, as exemplar.</b> A concrete solved problem that students work through and then imitate. Kuhn said this was the sense he cared most about."}, {"t": "attributional model of discovery", "d": "Brannigan's claim that discoveries are not events in themselves but events retrospectively recognised as origins. Mendel discovered his laws in 1866 as a result of what happened in 1900.", "w": 4, "f": ["attributional model of discovery"]}, {"t": "the scientification of knowledge", "d": "The assumption that only the products of Western science count as knowledge, which is how Indigenous and experiential knowing gets discredited as folklore.", "w": 6, "f": ["the scientification of knowledge"]}, {"t": "science illiteracy as resistance", "d": "Some people know the scientific explanation and choose another. Barr and Birke, and Martin on menstruation. The claim is narrow and the footnote says so.", "w": 7, "f": ["science illiteracy as resistance"]}, {"t": "the leper and the plague victim", "d": "Two techniques. The leper is excluded in one binary cut. The plague victim is included and sorted. Modern institutions inherit the second.", "w": 3, "f": ["the leper and the plague victim"]}, {"t": "spontaneous feminist empiricism", "d": "Harding's name for the view that the standard methods are fine and bias is a failure of execution. Keeps both traditional kinds of objectivity.", "w": 6, "f": ["spontaneous feminist empiricism"]}, {"t": "the unofficial PhD programme", "d": "The second curriculum that runs beside the official one: study groups, informal mentoring, the conversation in the corridor. Being socially included in it does not guarantee being technically included in it.", "w": 4, "f": ["the unofficial PhD programme"]}, {"t": "self-sufficiency of the mind", "d": "What she tells her son to cultivate, and what the ethics of belief requires: thoughts that belong to you, free of national partisanship and anybody else's authority.", "w": 7, "f": ["self-sufficiency of the mind"]}, {"t": "tautological transformation", "d": "Their description of all inference. The conclusion never contains anything the premises did not, so thinking alone cannot produce a new fact.", "w": 2, "f": ["tautological transformation"]}, {"t": "feminism, as they define it", "d": "A political movement for ending the arrangement by which societies sort people into two classes with different roles, in a way that reliably gives men more power. They call this minimalist, meaning the smallest version almost every feminist would sign.", "w": 5, "f": ["feminism, as they define it"]}, {"t": "the featureless abstraction", "d": "Lorraine Code's description of how philosophers treat the knower. Reducing a person to a variable is a decision that their features do not matter, taken before any argument for it.", "w": 5, "f": ["the featureless abstraction"]}, {"t": "feminist radical empiricism", "d": "The stronger claim that values are themselves empirical, so evidence bears on them and they can serve as evidence. Nelson, Clough, Anderson. Behind it is Quine's <b>web of belief</b>.", "w": 6, "f": ["feminist radical empiricism"]}, {"t": "the invention of invention", "d": "Invention becoming a deliberate, coherent project in medieval Europe. Everywhere else it stayed a slow accumulation of lucky finds.", "w": 2, "f": ["the invention of invention"]}, {"t": "the Mary and Martha switch", "d": "The Greek East read Luke 10 as ranking contemplation above action. The West inverted it, and by Eckhart's sermons Martha is the mature sister.", "w": 2, "f": ["the Mary and Martha switch"]}, {"t": "neo-Kantian constructivism", "d": "The strongest sense: when scientists agree on a claim they literally make it true. Sismondo rejects it, because it requires representation to act at a distance with no causal connection.", "w": 4, "f": ["neo-Kantian constructivism"]}, {"t": "methodological objectivity", "d": "Knowledge comes from reliable methods that anyone could run and get the same answer. The procedure is what makes it trustworthy.", "w": 6, "f": ["methodological objectivity"]}, {"t": "the three jobs the VFI did", "d": "It made diversity a question with no scientific stake, turned documented bias into isolated bad science, and made who benefits somebody else's department. Intemann's reason feminists attacked it, where correcting it would have done for anybody else.", "w": 7, "f": ["the three jobs the VFI did"]}, {"t": "the value-management model", "d": "Rolin, from Longino. Any values may enter, and a sufficiently diverse and critical community catches the damaging ones. Objectivity is a property of how the community is run.", "w": 7, "f": ["the value-management model"]}, {"t": "parental investment theory", "d": "Trivers 1972. The sex that invests more in offspring will be choosier, because of <b>gametic dimorphism</b>, the difference in size and number between sperm and eggs. The slogan is sperm is cheap and eggs are expensive.", "w": 7, "f": ["parental investment theory", "parental investment"]}, {"t": "Idols of the Market-place", "d": "From language. Words take their meanings from ordinary trade and talk, so bad words force bad thinking, and definitions cannot escape it because they are made of more words. Bacon thinks these are the worst.", "w": 2, "f": ["Idols of the Market-place"]}, {"t": "hierarchy of the sciences", "d": "Mathematics, astronomy, physics, chemistry, biology, sociology. Rising complexity, falling generality, each depending on the ones before.", "w": 2, "f": ["hierarchy of the sciences"]}, {"t": "heterogeneous engineering", "d": "Law's term. Building an artefact and building the environment in which it can work are one job, because neither can be done alone.", "w": 4, "f": ["heterogeneous engineering"]}, {"t": "the value-free ideal", "w": 5, "f": ["the value-free ideal, VFI", "the value-free ideal"], "d": "<b>the value-free ideal.</b> Good science as science with no preferences or politics in it anywhere. Set against the position that this field should be openly shaped by feminist aims. <b>the value-free ideal, VFI.</b> Good science has no social, political or moral commitments in it anywhere. Every position in this chapter rejects it, and almost none give up objectivity."}, {"t": "the supplement of virtue", "d": "His name for politeness, taste and style: things learning produces that stand in for goodness and never become it.", "w": 2, "f": ["the supplement of virtue"]}, {"t": "transformative criticism", "d": "Longino's four norms: public venues for criticism, real uptake, public standards, and tempered equality of intellectual authority.", "w": 6, "f": ["transformative criticism"]}, {"t": "the social justice model", "d": "Publicly funded science should aim at benefiting everyone and especially the worst off. It prioritises some values before the argument starts, where management lets all values compete.", "w": 7, "f": ["the social justice model"]}, {"t": "power of mind over mind", "d": "Bentham's own phrase for the effect.", "w": 3, "f": ["power of mind over mind"]}, {"t": "rational reconstruction", "d": "What a scientific paper is, as against a record of what happened. Details cleaned up, idiosyncrasies removed, order of events rearranged to make the argument work. Why citation counts measure persuasion, and not influence.", "w": 4, "f": ["rational reconstruction"]}, {"t": "feminisation and status", "d": "Rossiter's finding that a field's status falls as women enter it and rises as men do, which is why women could enter science most easily through new fields that were then poorly regarded.", "w": 4, "f": ["feminisation and status"]}, {"t": "biological essentialism", "d": "The view that some biological fact makes a person a woman and settles what she is like. The authors reject it in one sentence on page 4.", "w": 5, "f": ["biological essentialism"]}, {"t": "feminist epistemology", "d": "The study of knowledge, asked by way of whether who the knower is changes what gets known, with the aim of making knowledge-production better and stopping it failing people. Not a claim that women know differently.", "w": 5, "f": ["feminist epistemologies", "feminist epistemology"]}, {"t": "standpoint theory", "d": "Harding and Wylie. Some social positions give a better view of how a system works, usually the positions the system acts on, and this can be used deliberately. The term in the week's title.", "w": 5, "f": ["standpoint empiricism", "standpoint theorists", "standpoint theorist", "standpoint theory"]}, {"t": "contextual empiricism", "d": "Longino. Nothing is evidence by itself: a fact becomes evidence for a hypothesis only through background assumptions that connect them.", "w": 6, "f": ["contextual empiricism", "contextual empiricist"]}, {"t": "the five entry points", "d": "Framing the problem, observing and describing, value-laden concepts and risk, choosing models, interpreting data and weighing alternatives.", "w": 7, "f": ["the five entry points"]}, {"t": "the Form of the Good", "d": "The sun outside the cave. Last and hardest to see, and the cause of everything correct and beautiful.", "w": 2, "f": ["the Form of the Good"]}, {"t": "Idols of the Theatre", "d": "Inherited philosophical systems, which he calls stage-plays: tidy invented worlds performed for an audience that mistakes them for the world.", "w": 2, "f": ["Idols of the Theatre"]}, {"t": "incommensurability", "d": "No common measure between two paradigms. The words change meaning, the good questions change, and the standards for a good answer change, so the evidence cannot simply be totted up.", "w": 3, "f": ["incommensurabilities", "incommensurability", "incommensurable"]}, {"t": "cumulative advantage", "d": "The general form of the same idea, and the reason Sismondo likes it: differences too small to call unfair at any one step end up producing differences large enough to be undeniable.", "w": 4, "f": ["cumulative advantage"]}, {"t": "confounding variable", "d": "A factor other than the one being studied that moves with the behaviour being measured, so it can mask or imitate a real effect. Naming one is what lets you control for it.", "w": 6, "f": ["confounding variable"]}, {"t": "the inversion thesis", "d": "The engine of standpoint theory. Those who are subordinated are, because of that position, better placed to see the assumptions researchers in dominant positions are making.", "w": 6, "f": ["the inversion thesis"]}, {"t": "non-epistemic values", "d": "Moral, political, social and economic values, held to be distinct from the aims of knowing. The VFI is about keeping these out, and it grants that some values, such as valuing prediction or empirical adequacy, are part of the enterprise.", "w": 7, "f": ["non-epistemic values"]}, {"t": "Longino’s four norms", "d": "Recognised avenues for criticism; shared standards critics can appeal to; a community that responds to criticism; intellectual authority shared equally among qualified practitioners.", "w": 7, "f": ["Longino’s four norms"]}, {"t": "manufactured dissent", "d": "Privately funded researchers raising objections in order to stall policy, confuse the public and divert other scientists' time. The Merchants of Doubt strategy, named as an obstacle to the management model.", "w": 7, "f": ["manufactured dissent"]}, {"t": "the ethics of belief", "d": "Believing is something you do, so it can be done responsibly or not. Named by Clifford in 1877; practised by Du Ch&acirc;telet from the 1730s.", "w": 7, "f": ["the ethics of belief"]}, {"t": "law of three states", "d": "Every branch of knowledge passes through theological, then metaphysical, then positive.", "w": 2, "f": ["law of three states"]}, {"t": "empirical statement", "d": "About the world, checkable against experience, always capable of turning out wrong.", "w": 2, "f": ["empirical statement"]}, {"t": "origin essentialism", "d": "Treating where a thing came from as part of what it is. Respectable when understood as protecting rights that grew out of a history.", "w": 2, "f": ["origin essentialism"]}, {"t": "situated knowledge", "d": "Knowledge held from a particular position. The claim the book defends is that partial is not false: a view from somewhere is still a view of something real.", "w": 5, "f": ["situated knowledges", "situated knowledge", "situatedness"]}, {"t": "epistemic injustice", "d": "A wrong done to somebody as a knower: being denied what you need to make sense of your own experience, or not being taken seriously when you report it.", "w": 5, "f": ["epistemic injustice"]}, {"t": "epistemic advantage", "d": "What standpoint claims for marginalised knowers. It is never automatic and never comes from identity alone: it requires conscious awareness of the power relations making up that position.", "w": 6, "f": ["epistemic advantage"]}, {"t": "underdetermination", "d": "The evidence never picks out one theory on its own, because several fit the same data. What <b>gap feminist empiricism</b> says values are for.", "w": 6, "f": ["underdeterminations", "underdetermination", "underdetermined"]}, {"t": "the five strategies", "d": "How ignorance gets made: neglect, suppression, discrediting, disinformation, selective representation.", "w": 6, "f": ["the five strategies"]}, {"t": "hypothesis, in 1740", "d": "An <b>unobservable mechanism proposed to account for what you can see</b>, which is not the modern sense of a guess you go and test. Hawking radiation is one in this sense: nobody has observed it, and it is proposed because the theory needs it. In 1740 the case everybody argued about was Descartes's <b>vortices</b>, his claim that space is packed with invisible swirling matter which carries the planets round. Whether a scientist may propose one at all is this chapter's quarrel.", "w": 7, "f": ["hypothesis, in 1740", "hypotheses", "hypothesis"]}, {"t": "the primate pattern", "d": "What the 1960s savannah baboon studies proposed: male dominance, a division of labour by sex, and male aggression, taken as typical of primates and read across to humans.", "w": 7, "f": ["the primate pattern"]}, {"t": "Idols of the Tribe", "d": "Built into the species. We see more order than there is, count the hits and forget the misses, and treat human senses as the measure of things.", "w": 2, "f": ["Idols of the Tribe"]}, {"t": "metaphysical state", "d": "The wills are replaced by abstract forces with no personality. Peaks in one entity, Nature. Transitional only.", "w": 2, "f": ["metaphysical state"]}, {"t": "the vicious circle", "d": "You cannot observe usefully without a theory, and cannot have a grounded theory without observing. Theology broke the deadlock by giving thought something to hold.", "w": 2, "f": ["the vicious circle"]}, {"t": "analytic statement", "d": "The truths of logic and mathematics. True because of what the words mean, carrying no information about the world.", "w": 2, "f": ["analytic statement"]}, {"t": "synthetic a priori", "d": "Kant's third category: claims both informative about the world and knowable before any experience. The Circle denies the category exists.", "w": 2, "f": ["synthetic a priori"]}, {"t": "a diagram of power", "d": "Foucault's key move. The Panopticon is a mechanism stripped of any particular use, detachable from prisons and droppable into schools, clinics and workshops.", "w": 3, "f": ["a diagram of power"]}, {"t": "the Matthew Effect", "d": "Merton's name, from Matthew 13:12, for the way scientific advantage compounds. A small early edge produces a better mentor, a better department, more visibility, more citations, and citation is itself a reward that produces more work.", "w": 4, "f": ["the Matthew Effect"]}, {"t": "creating phenomena", "d": "The laboratory sense of construction: materials arrive purified and standardised, objects are put in artificial situations, and new stable objects of study are manufactured. Nature is excluded from the laboratory, never brought into it.", "w": 4, "f": ["creating phenomena"]}, {"t": "significant truths", "d": "Research chases the truths somebody thinks are worth having, which is a much smaller set than the true things. Heart disease in men is the case.", "w": 5, "f": ["significant truths"]}, {"t": "strong objectivity", "d": "Harding. Scrutinising every phase, including whether the subject and questions suit the goals of the research. <b>Weak objectivity</b> is meeting the traditional standards of evidence, which covers phases 3 to 7.", "w": 6, "f": ["strong objectivity"]}, {"t": "Tuana’s five kinds", "d": "What we do not know but do not care to; what we do not know that we do not know; what they do not want us to know; what we do not know and do not want to; what we cannot know.", "w": 6, "f": ["Tuana’s five kinds"]}, {"t": "willful ignorance", "d": "The fourth kind. Active strategies to avoid knowing, including neglecting evidence. Distinct from <b>self-deception</b>, which is convincing yourself of something you want to be true.", "w": 6, "f": ["willfully ignorant", "willful ignorance"]}, {"t": "democratic science", "d": "Broader participation in knowledge production. Her warning is that the phrase is used by do-it-yourself biology spaces whose members are already scientifically trained and mostly want freedom to become entrepreneurs.", "w": 7, "f": ["democratic science"]}, {"t": "Idols of the Cave", "d": "The private den each person has: temperament, upbringing, what you read, whom you admire.", "w": 2, "f": ["Idols of the Cave"]}, {"t": "theological state", "d": "Explanation names a will behind the event. Peaks in monotheism.", "w": 2, "f": ["theological state"]}, {"t": "origin chauvinism", "d": "The same belief taken as a claim about intrinsic properties, marking a real difference between two things nobody can tell apart. Dennett calls this pernicious nonsense.", "w": 2, "f": ["origin chauvinism"]}, {"t": "epistemology", "d": "The study of knowledge: what knowing is, how it differs from believing, and what makes one claim better supported than another.", "w": 5, "f": ["epistemologically", "epistemological", "epistemologies", "epistemically", "epistemology", "epistemic"]}, {"t": "agent objectivity", "d": "The researcher should be detached, with no stake and no values in play. The person is what makes it trustworthy.", "w": 6, "f": ["agent objectivity"]}, {"t": "epistemic virtues", "d": "What makes a claim good as knowledge: empirical adequacy, inferential robustness, explanatory power, internal coherence, consistency with established knowledge. Wylie's point is that you cannot maximise them all at once.", "w": 6, "f": ["epistemic virtues"]}, {"t": "the deficit model", "d": "The assumption that the public has a hole where knowledge should be, and that filling it produces support for science. The thing science studies has spent decades pushing against.", "w": 7, "f": ["the deficit model"]}, {"t": "medical apartheid", "d": "Washington. Some women, primarily Black women, have been used for the benefit of other women's health, and the separation is necessary to the arrangement, and never accidental. Her reason women cannot be treated as one group.", "w": 7, "f": ["medical apartheid"]}, {"t": "gender stereotype", "d": "A generalisation attributing a characteristic to all members of a group when it is untrue of many or most. Nelson's case: females tied to reproduction, which then looks natural and so not in need of study.", "w": 7, "f": ["gender stereotype"]}, {"t": "sexual selection", "d": "Darwin's second mechanism, built to explain traits that hurt survival, such as the peacock's train. Two processes: males compete for access, and females choose.", "w": 7, "f": ["sexually selected", "sexual selection"]}, {"t": "the divided line", "d": "Plato's four rungs, low to high: imaging, belief, thought, knowledge. The bottom two are opinion, the top two intellect.", "w": 2, "f": ["the divided line"]}, {"t": "philosopher-king", "d": "The claim that a city should be governed by the people least eager to govern, because anyone who wants power wants it for themselves.", "w": 2, "f": ["philosopher-king"]}, {"t": "logical analysis", "d": "The method: take a statement apart to see whether anything in it can be cashed out in experience.", "w": 2, "f": ["logical analysis"]}, {"t": "normal science", "d": "What scientists do nearly all the time: solving puzzles inside a framework that is not itself being tested. A failure counts against the scientist.", "w": 3, "f": ["normal scientist", "normal science"]}, {"t": "the bias paradox", "d": "Feminists reject research for carrying values while endorsing feminist values. The escape: they never claimed values are bad as such, and the complaint is that the sexist ones went unexamined.", "w": 6, "f": ["the bias paradox"]}, {"t": "loving ignorance", "d": "Lugones and Ortega. Accepting that some experience cannot be fully shared, while refusing to ignore or stereotype the people whose it is. Reading about it is not enough and produces its own arrogance.", "w": 6, "f": ["loving ignorance"]}, {"t": "epistemic bubble", "d": "Nguyen. Where access to counter-evidence is limited or discouraged, built out of cognitive bias, trusted networks and recommendation algorithms.", "w": 6, "f": ["epistemic bubble"]}, {"t": "theory-ladenness", "d": "Observation is shaped by background assumptions, and those assumptions can be social. Martin on the passive egg and the burrowing sperm.", "w": 7, "f": ["theory-ladenness", "theory laden", "theory-laden"]}, {"t": "Salomon's House", "d": "The state research institute in Bacon's <em>New Atlantis</em>, with a division of labour between the people who gather experiments and the people who reason from them.", "w": 2, "f": ["Salomon's House"]}, {"t": "the Final Reply", "d": "The last of Rousseau's answers to two years of rebuttals of his prize-winning discourse, which is why it is so compressed.", "w": 2, "f": ["the Final Reply"]}, {"t": "unified science", "d": "The goal: one body of knowledge with no separate philosophical province standing above it.", "w": 2, "f": ["unified science"]}, {"t": "the plague town", "d": "The seventeenth-century lockdown Foucault opens with. Registers, daily roll calls, written reports upward. He calls it a compact model of the disciplinary mechanism.", "w": 3, "f": ["the plague town"]}, {"t": "stratification", "d": "The fact that science is layered: a small minority of people produce most of the papers and receive most of the citations. Roughly 10 per cent of authors produce half of all papers.", "w": 4, "f": ["stratifications", "stratification", "stratified"]}, {"t": "the Hrdy result", "d": "Female primates are often as promiscuous as males, and the advantage runs the other way: several males who might be the father are several males with a reason to protect the infant.", "w": 5, "f": ["the Hrdy result"]}, {"t": "social location", "d": "The historical, political and cultural position a knower is actually in, including gender, race, class, sexuality and ability. Traditionally held to be irrelevant to knowing.", "w": 5, "f": ["social location"]}, {"t": "the nine phases", "d": "Choosing the subject, framing the questions, deciding what is relevant, collecting, interpreting, deciding when to stop, concluding, considering alternatives, testing robustness. They loop and they feed each other.", "w": 6, "f": ["the nine phases"]}, {"t": "outsider-within", "d": "Collins. A marginalised researcher holds the field's framework and the lived experience of those studied at once, and the mismatch is what they can see. <b>Double consciousness</b> is the name for holding both.", "w": 6, "f": ["outsider-within"]}, {"t": "white ignorance", "d": "Mills. Not a gap but a refusal to understand the conditions non-white people live under, maintained because the dominant group has an interest in the world looking fair.", "w": 6, "f": ["white ignorance"]}, {"t": "micro-practices", "d": "Altmann's term for the business of the female baboons, which she argued drives the group's social dynamics more than the high drama between males.", "w": 7, "f": ["micro-practices"]}, {"t": "true induction", "d": "Tables of presence, absence and degree; eliminate causes using the negative cases; climb in small steps toward general statements.", "w": 2, "f": ["true induction"]}, {"t": "positive state", "d": "Causes and purposes abandoned as unanswerable. Asks only for laws, meaning invariable relations of succession and resemblance.", "w": 2, "f": ["positive state"]}, {"t": "the Panopticon", "d": "Bentham's 1787 design: a ring of backlit cells around a dark central tower, so the inmate is seen and can never check whether anyone is looking.", "w": 3, "f": ["the Panopticon"]}, {"t": "primatology", "d": "The study of monkeys and apes. One of the fields women entered in numbers in the 1970s and 1980s.", "w": 5, "f": ["primatological", "primatologists", "primatologist", "primatology"]}, {"t": "the coy female", "d": "The settled view before that: males promiscuous, females picky and monogamous, justified by both biology and evolutionary advantage. Hrdy's 1986 paper calls it a myth.", "w": 5, "f": ["the coy female"]}, {"t": "S knows that p", "d": "The standard notation of epistemology, where p is a claim and S is whoever knows it. All the argument goes into conditions on p, which is the complaint.", "w": 5, "f": ["S knows that p"]}, {"t": "inductive risk", "d": "Deciding you have enough evidence is a judgment about the cost of being wrong, which is a value judgment. Rudner 1953, revived by Douglas.", "w": 6, "f": ["inductive risk"]}, {"t": "Novum Organum", "d": "\"The new instrument\", 1620, named as a replacement for Aristotle's works on logic, which were called the <em>Organon</em>.", "w": 2, "f": ["Novum Organum"]}, {"t": "machina mundi", "d": "The world-machine. Once rejected on religious grounds, and used approvingly by thirteenth-century Latin clerical scientists.", "w": 2, "f": ["machina mundi"]}, {"t": "co-production", "d": "Science and social order shaping each other at the same time. The criteria for good climate science are shaped by policy while the criteria for good policy are shaped by climate science.", "w": 4, "f": ["co-production", "coproduction", "co-produced"]}, {"t": "the god trick", "d": "Donna Haraway's name for the pretence of seeing from everywhere and nowhere at once. <b>The view from nowhere</b> is Thomas Nagel's name for the same thing, and he meant it approvingly.", "w": 5, "f": ["the god trick"]}, {"t": "agnotology", "d": "The study of ignorance: what we do not know and why. Its claim is that ignorance is often produced, where it looks like something merely left over, and Oreskes and Conway on tobacco and climate is the standard case.", "w": 5, "f": ["agnotological", "agnotology"]}, {"t": "elevator word", "d": "Hacking. A word that does no work of its own and only raises the standing of whatever it is attached to. His charge against <em>objective</em>.", "w": 6, "f": ["elevator word"]}, {"t": "standing with", "d": "TallBear's alternative to community-based participatory research, which she argues keeps the boundary between inside and outside the academy in place.", "w": 7, "f": ["standing with"]}, {"t": "androcentrism", "d": "Male-centredness. Research that takes what males do as the main thing worth studying and does not properly attend to what females do.", "w": 7, "f": ["androcentrism", "androcentric"]}, {"t": "discipline", "d": "Power that works by sorting: dividing a crowd into individuals, giving each a place, a timetable and a record, then watching. Its operation is analysis, breaking a thing into parts.", "w": 3, "f": ["disciplinary", "disciplines", "discipline"]}, {"t": "the pipeline", "d": "The standard metaphor for women leaving science, with leaks at every joint. Sismondo accepts it only if intake, blockage and filters are attended to as well as leakage, since in many fields there are few women at the source to begin with.", "w": 4, "f": ["the pipeline"]}, {"t": "a posteriori", "d": "Knowable only by checking the world. The Earth is flattened at the poles is a posteriori, since it took the 1736 expedition to Lapland to settle it.", "w": 7, "f": ["a posteriori"]}, {"t": "dialectic", "d": "Argument that dismantles its own hypotheses and works back to a starting point needing nothing under it. For Plato, the only thing that counts as knowledge in the strict sense.", "w": 2, "f": ["dialectical", "dialectic"]}, {"t": "nominalism", "d": "The view that kinds are human impositions and only individual objects are real. Its opposite here is realism about kinds. Nominalism is one way of cashing out the construction metaphor.", "w": 4, "f": ["nominalists", "nominalism", "nominalist"]}, {"t": "relativism", "d": "The view that what is true depends entirely on who is asking. The objection the field exists under, and the thing most feminist epistemology is trying to avoid.", "w": 5, "f": ["relativists", "relativism", "relativist"]}, {"t": "studying up", "d": "Studying from the margins out: working with the affected group to frame the problem, identify everyone affected, and ask how power contributed to it.", "w": 7, "f": ["studying up"]}, {"t": "Scholastics", "d": "The medieval university philosophers, who settled questions by reasoning from Aristotle and from established authority. Their explanations name a power and stop there, as in opium puts you to sleep because it has a sleep-making virtue. Descartes attacks them for it, since naming the power tells you nothing about how the opium works.", "w": 7, "f": ["Scholastics"]}, {"t": "polyandrous", "d": "Of females who mate with more than one male. Hrdy's finding is that this is at the core of the breeding systems of most troop-dwelling primates.", "w": 7, "f": ["polyandrous", "polyandry"]}, {"t": "pleonexia", "d": "Wanting more than your share. The word behind \"greed\" in the <em>Republic</em>, read best as a direction of attention toward what can be consumed.", "w": 2, "f": ["pleonexias", "pleonexia"]}, {"t": "think tank", "d": "An organisation that employs researchers to publish reports and policy arguments, funded by whoever chose to fund it, with no students and no requirement to be peer reviewed. Its output looks like scholarship, which is what makes it useful for making a paid-for conclusion look independent.", "w": 5, "f": ["think tank"]}, {"t": "empeiría", "d": "Experience. You know <em>that</em> something works because you have seen it work, and you have no account of why.", "w": 2, "f": ["empeirias", "empeirías", "empeiria", "empeiría"]}, {"t": "epistēmē", "d": "Theoretical knowledge of what never changes, mathematics being the model. It produces nothing and Greek thought ranked it highest.", "w": 2, "f": ["epistemes", "epistēmēs", "episteme", "epistēmē"]}, {"t": "atechnía", "d": "Productive skill resting on a <b>false</b> idea of the thing itself. Translated as blunder. Bloodletting and the lobotomy are the examples.", "w": 2, "f": ["atechnias", "atechnías", "atechnia", "atechnía"]}, {"t": "mēchanē", "d": "A contrivance. English machine, mechanism, mechanical and machination come from it.", "w": 2, "f": ["mechanes", "mēchanēs", "mechane", "mēchanē"]}, {"t": "órganon", "d": "Originally a tool. From Plato onward applied to the parts of the body, which is why they are called organs.", "w": 2, "f": ["organons", "órganons", "organon", "órganon"]}, {"t": "deinótēs", "d": "From Sophocles. Literally \"terribleness\", holding <b>ability</b> and <b>monstrous power</b> in one word, with no way to take one without the other.", "w": 2, "f": ["deinotes", "deinótēs"]}, {"t": "Isotype", "d": "Otto Neurath's pictorial statistics, where a quantity is shown by repeating a small icon at a fixed size. The ancestor of every pictogram chart.", "w": 2, "f": ["Isotypes", "Isotype"]}, {"t": "dualism", "d": "The claim that mind is made of a different kind of stuff from body. Dennett's objection is that nothing coherent can be said about where the two meet.", "w": 2, "f": ["dualists", "dualism", "dualist"]}, {"t": "vitalism", "d": "The nineteenth-century claim that living matter contains a special life-force. Dead, because biochemistry showed those powers come out of ordinary chemistry.", "w": 2, "f": ["vitalism", "vitalist"]}, {"t": "realism", "d": "The intuition that truths depend more on the natural world than on the people stating them. Sismondo notes it is usually a negative position, defined against whichever anti-realism is in front of it.", "w": 4, "f": ["realists", "realism", "realist"]}, {"t": "a priori", "d": "Knowable without checking the world. Every bachelor is unmarried is a priori, and so is a sum in arithmetic.", "w": 7, "f": ["a priori"]}, {"t": "vortices", "d": "Descartes's account of what fills space. There is no vacuum, so the gaps between the planets are packed with invisible matter, and it turns in whirlpools that carry the planets round. Nobody can see one, which is what made it the standard example of a <b>hypothesis</b> in the 1740 sense.", "w": 7, "f": ["vortices"]}, {"t": "technē", "d": "Craft knowledge. Plato's word for practical, productive know-how, which he says works without being able to give an account of itself.", "w": 2, "f": ["technes", "technēs", "techne", "technē"]}, {"t": "physis", "d": "The Greek word English translates as \"nature\". It names a process, a growing, and never a set of objects. Aristotle: whatever has the source of its own motion inside itself.", "w": 2, "f": ["physes", "physis"]}, {"t": "týchē", "d": "Chance, happy coincidence. Aristotle quotes a poet saying technē loves happy chance and happy chance loves technē.", "w": 2, "f": ["tyches", "týchēs", "tyche", "týchē"]}];
